(Plan 310 task 6 - a print now asks whether the name in front of it holds a
 thing, so the table that answers has to be exactly as scoped as the
 declarations are. A boolean declared inside a function shadows a top-level
 thing of the same name for the length of that function, and printing it must
 print the boolean - not read sixteen more bytes off the stack as though a
 stamp were sitting there.

 The second function proves the same table the other way: a local thing of a
 different shape prints as its own shape, and the top-level stamp is whole
 again once both functions have returned.

 Acceptance: the boolean, the sentence it guards, the package, then the
 top-level stamp.)

A thing called stamp has
  a number called 'day sent' is 25,
  a number called 'cost in pence' is 12.

A thing called package has
  a stamp called stamp,
  a number called 'weight in grams' is 20.

a stamp called posted.

To 'check the letter went out'.
  a boolean called posted is true.
  Print posted.
  If posted then,
    Print "the letter went out".

To 'weigh what was sent'.
  a package called posted.
  Print posted.

'check the letter went out'.
'weigh what was sent'.
Print posted.
